PREFIX dblp: <https://dblp.example/voc#>
PREFIX xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>

# An author, and the co-authorship relation is a SELF-EDGE — `@dblp:Person`,
# not a literal — because that is the only shape the layout pass reads. The
# adjacency it runs Louvain over is the `by_source` file of a relation whose
# two endpoints are the SAME vertex type (`fossil-cli/src/host.rs::
# enrich_written_layout`), so an intermediate `Coauthorship` node would give a
# corpus whose `cluster_id` came from an empty graph.
#
# ## Why `;` and not `*`, which is a DEFECT and not a modelling choice
#
# The honest cardinality here is `*`: an author has many co-authors. Writing it
# does not work. `fossil-mir/src/lower.rs` turns a non-`Single` shape
# cardinality into `EmitEdge { single_valued: false }`, and
# `fossil-df/src/lib.rs::materialise_edge` reads that as «the destination is a
# List» and calls `unnest_columns(["dst_iri"])` unconditionally — which is true
# of an `io.rdf` pivot and false of every tabular source, where `dst_iri` is a
# scalar. The run dies in DataFusion:
#
#     execute: Internal error: trying to unnest on invalid data type Utf8.
#
# So a multi-valued edge cannot currently be written from a CSV at all. It is
# left alone here — this branch measures the corpus, it does not repair the
# writer.
#
# The workaround is sound for what is being measured. `single_valued` reaches
# exactly two places: that `unnest`, and the `cardinality:` field the manifest
# declares. It does NOT filter rows — nothing collapses the edges to one per
# source — so the corpus carries all 2,099,732 of them, one per input row, and
# the only casualty is a manifest that says `single` about a relation that is
# not. Every count below is verified against SNAP's own header after the run.
dblp:Person {
  dblp:aid      xsd:integer ;
  dblp:coauthor @dblp:Person
}
